<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Nung Autonomous Territory</h1><p class="st-meta">1947 CE – 1954 CE · capital Mong Cai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A French-backed autonomous zone of the Nung people in north-eastern Vietnam during the First Indochina War, led by Voong A Sang.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1947 French authorities, seeking allies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a>, recognised an autonomous territory in the Hai Ninh area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mong-cai">Mong Cai</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nung-people">Nung</a>, as the largely Chinese-speaking border communities there were known. The local leader Voong A Sang, who had built up a militia, became its head.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory was administered under French supervision with its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nung-people">Nung</a> armed units, which fought alongside French forces. It was one of several ethnic autonomous areas, including the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tai-federation">Tai Federation</a>, created as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France's</a> strategy of dividing Vietnamese nationalism. After 1950 it was formally placed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bao-dai">Bao Dai's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-vietnam">State of Vietnam</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Geneva</a> Accords of 1954 placed the area in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">North Vietnam</a>. Voong A Sang and thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nung-people">Nung</a> moved south, where Nung units served in the Army of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-vietnam">Republic of Vietnam</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nung-autonomous-territory.jpg" alt="Nung Autonomous Territory" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: The government of the Nùng Autonomous Territory (Original design)Donald Trung Quoc Don (徵國單) made the SVG file. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_N%C3%B9ng_Autonomous_Territory.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1947 CE – 1954 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mong Cai</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Voong A Sang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Folk religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chinese (Hakka, Cantonese), Nung</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khu tự trị Nùng</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1950&amp;focus=nung-autonomous-territory" aria-label="Nung Autonomous Territory on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7518 1369 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nung Autonomous Territory lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7838 1625h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7838 1625h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Vietnam. <a href="/atlas?year=1950&amp;focus=nung-autonomous-territory">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

